-- grover.42 -- Grover's search, and the clearest small picture of what an
-- algorithm actually does with the arrows.
--
-- THE PROBLEM.  One of N possibilities is the one you want, and nothing
-- distinguishes it except a sealed test that says yes to it and no to the rest.
-- Classically there is nothing to do but try them, N/2 tries on average.
-- Grover's algorithm finds it in about sqrt(N).
--
-- WHAT MAKES IT WORK is not that all N are tried at once.  That much is free
-- and worth nothing: look at a uniform spread and you see one possibility at
-- random, which is guessing.  The work is done by cancellation, and the two
-- halves of the loop divide it:
--
--   the oracle    turns the arrow of the marked possibility to point backwards.
--                 THIS IS INVISIBLE.  Every outcome still has the same chance
--                 as before; only a direction changed.
--   the diffusion reflects every arrow about their average, which converts that
--                 invisible difference into a visible one.
--
-- Run `stage1`, `stage2` and `grover11` in turn on |00> to watch exactly that:
-- four equal arrows, then four equal arrows with one turned round, then all of
-- it on |11>.
--
-- TWO QUBITS IS EXACT.  With N = 4 one turn of the crank gives the answer with
-- certainty, which is the smallest interesting case in quantum computing.
-- THREE IS NOT: eight possibilities want two turns, and land at 94.5%.  Note
-- what that means for a language with no loop -- the number of turns is fixed by
-- the width of the register, so it is known before the program runs, and the
-- crank is written out rather than iterated.  See DRAFT-Q42.md section 4.2.

type qubit = 1 + 1

def x      = swapsum
def s      = id + (omega ; omega)
def z      = id + (omega ; omega ; omega ; omega)
def hcore  = x ; s ; v ; s ; x
def h      = unitprod! ; (omega * hcore) ; unitprod
def mat    = dist ; (unitprod + unitprod)
def ctrl m = mat ; (id + m) ; mat!


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Two qubits.  `cz` flips the sign of |11> and leaves the rest alone, so it is
-- already the oracle for that one; conjugating with `x` moves the mark.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def h2      = h * h
def cz      = ctrl z

def mark00  = (x * x)  ; cz ; (x * x)
def mark01  = (x * id) ; cz ; (x * id)
def mark10  = (id * x) ; cz ; (id * x)
def mark11  = cz

-- Reflect about the average: spread, flip the sign of |00>, unspread.  This is
-- the Hadamard sandwich -- the reflection is trivial to state in one basis and
-- is what you want in the other, and `h2` is what carries you between them.
def diffuse = h2 ; mark00 ; h2

def grover00 = h2 ; mark00 ; diffuse
def grover01 = h2 ; mark01 ; diffuse
def grover10 = h2 ; mark10 ; diffuse
def grover11 = h2 ; mark11 ; diffuse

-- The three stages, for watching.
def stage1 = h2                     -- 0.5|00> + 0.5|01> + 0.5|10> + 0.5|11>
def stage2 = h2 ; mark11            -- the same four chances, one arrow reversed


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Three qubits, where one turn is no longer enough.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def h3       = h * (h * h)
def ccz      = ctrl cz
def mark000  = (x * (x * x)) ; ccz ; (x * (x * x))
def diffuse3 = h3 ; mark000 ; h3
def step3    = ccz ; diffuse3

def once3    = h3 ; step3           -- 78.1% on |111>
def grover3  = h3 ; step3 ; step3   -- 94.5% on |111>
